   "section": "**Part (c)**",
   "text": "With a laminar leading edge and Retrans = 5 × 10 <sup>5</sup>, Eq. (7.49a) applies:\n\nThe drag can be recomputed for this lower drag coefficient:"
  },
  {
   "chunk_id": "DOC_FLUID_MECHANICS_7TH_ED_MCGRAW_HILL_SERIES_IN_MECHANICAL_ENGI#c01131",
   "page_start": 846,
   "page_end": 846,
   "section": "**Part (d)**",
   "text": "Finally, for the rough wall, we calculate\n\nFrom Fig. 7.6 at Re _L_ = 4.36 × 10 <sup>6</sup>, this condition is just inside the fully rough\nregime. Equation (7.48 _b_ ) applies:\n\n_CD_ = (1.89 + 1.62 log 3000) <sup>-2.5</sup> = 0.00644\n\nand the drag estimate is\n\nThis small roughness nearly doubles the drag. It is probable that the total\nhydrofoil drag is still another factor of 2 larger because of trailing-edge flow\nseparation effects."
  },
  {
   "chunk_id": "DOC_FLUID_MECHANICS_7TH_ED_MCGRAW_HILL_SERIES_IN_MECHANICAL_ENGI#c01132",
   "page_start": 846,
   "page_end": 846,
   "section": "**7.5 Boundary Layers with Pressure Gradient3**",
   "text": "The flat-plate analysis of the previous section should give us a good feeling\nfor the behavior of both laminar and turbulent boundary layers, except for one\nimportant effect: flow separation. Prandtl showed that separation like that in Fig."
